Subject: [PATCH 37/44] =?UTF-8?q?feat(chexc):=20R3=20=E2=80=94=20one=20can?=
 =?UTF-8?q?onical=20form=20for=20capability=20lists,=20in=20type=20identit?=
 =?UTF-8?q?y=20too?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The semantic form was satisfied behaviourally (every comparison is coverage-based) but not in
identity: 'FuncTy' kept the raw spelling it was built with, and interning keys on that. The review
found the observer -- 'Box<() throws (Base, Derived) -> Unit>' would not go where
'Box<() throws Base -> Unit>' is expected, since invariance compares identity and never consults
subtyping.

Normalization now runs at the four boundaries where a list is PRODUCED, never inside interning
(the 2026-08-22 attempt did that and broke 17 tests: coverage needs 'IsSubtype', which re-enters
type formation): clause elaboration for declarations and for functional type nodes, generic
substitution, and the inferred write-back; the '.cjo' loader gets it too, so a file from an older
compiler cannot smuggle a raw list in. Accessor lists normalize after instantiation for the same
reason. 'NormalizeCapTys' also drops a concrete entry whose type is unchecked, which is the rule
of "Unchecked types in 'throws' lists" reaching identity: 'safeThrow<Unchecked>' and the
clause-free shape are now literally one type, where the 2026-08-30 fix had only made them
assignable.

One regression on the way, worth the comment it got: with lists legitimately emptying by
normalization, 'HandleCall's defence-in-depth fallback started firing and re-derived the callee's
DECLARED list -- uninstantiated, so a call that pins 'E' to an unchecked type demanded
'CanThrow<E>' again. The fallback now applies the call's own type mapping and normalizes, which is
what it should have done from the start.

Gate: compiler/Sema 3,502/3,502 (where the earlier attempt failed), IncrementalCompilation
1,944/1,944, std and stdx build clean at 'warn'.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
